    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mutsuhito came to the throne as a boy in 1867, and in his name the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and Chōshū <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> and proclaimed the restoration of imperial rule in 1868.</li><li>His reign saw the domains abolished, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>'s privileges ended, a cabinet, constitution and Diet created, and victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1895 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1910.</li><li>How much power he held himself is disputed: he kept no diary and wrote almost no letters, and historians call him anything from a figurehead to an autocrat.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Meiji's waka asking why wind and waves rise in discord when the seas of the four directions are all born of one womb was recited by his grandson Emperor Shōwa at an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-conference">Imperial Conference</a> in September 1941, before the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

